-- Passage rg12-story-details-inference-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('37cd2a2f-789d-5ae3-882f-1bcb1ddcd067', 'rg12-story-details-inference-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Offer on Board Six$q$, $q$By the fourth hour, the cafeteria had emptied of parents, and only two of the six boards along the far wall still had pieces on them. Nadia Haddad sat at board six with eleven minutes left on her clock. Her opponent, a tall boy from Westbrook named Tobias Lindqvist, had nearly forty.

She had won a pawn in the opening and kept it, trading pieces whenever she could, the way Coach Kim had drilled them all season. On paper she was winning. In practice she had spent too long on move twenty, and now every move cost her time she did not have.

Across the room, the match scoreboard read Ridgeview 2.5, Westbrook 1.5. Board three was still going, and from where Nadia sat she could see Marcus leaning forward with both hands pressed against his cheeks, which was what he did when he was losing.

Tobias moved his rook, pressed his clock, and said quietly, "Draw?"

Nadia did not answer at once. A draw would give her team half a point. If Marcus lost, the match would end in a tie; if he held on to even a draw, Ridgeview would win. A win on her own board would settle everything, but only if she found the right moves with eleven minutes, now ten.

She looked at the board for a long time. Then she looked at board three. Then she reached out and shook Tobias's hand.

Afterward, on the bus, Marcus slid into the seat beside her. He had lost, and the match had ended in a tie. "You were a pawn up," he said. "You could have won that."

"Maybe," Nadia said. She was setting up the final position on her travel set, and she kept moving the pieces forward and back, trying lines she had not had time for during the game. She did not say whether she had found the win, and Marcus, after watching her for a while, did not ask again.$q$, 'draft')
