-- Passage rg12-story-details-inference-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0eeda0be-76cb-58e3-b418-43df3bd2282e', 'rg12-story-details-inference-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Two Columns$q$, $q$The sampler had hung in the front hall of the Fairhaven Historical Society for as long as the society had existed. Its label, typed on a yellowed card, said it was stitched in 1846 by Clara Whitcombe, age eleven, daughter of the town's first schoolmaster. Visitors stopped to admire the tiny alphabet, the rows of numbers, and the careful border of strawberries.

Mei-Lin Zhou, a high school intern, had been asked to help take it down for cleaning. Ms. Achterberg, the conservator, wore cotton gloves and showed Mei-Lin how to support the frame from below so the old linen would not shift.

When they removed the backing board, Mei-Lin saw the reverse of the stitching for the first time. Most of it was what she expected: knots, crossings, short tails of faded thread. But in the lower corner, hidden behind where the frame had been, someone had stitched two small letters in blue: A.R.

"Clara's initials would be C.W.," Mei-Lin said.

"They would," Ms. Achterberg agreed. She bent closer with a magnifying lamp. "And look at the strawberries along the bottom border. The stitches on the left half are tight and even. On the right half they are a little looser, and the thread is pulled in a different direction."

Mei-Lin looked from the border to the blue letters and back. "So someone else finished it?"

"Possibly. Or someone else started it. Or A.R. is a teacher's mark, or a later repair." Ms. Achterberg straightened. "What we know is what we can see. What we think goes in a different column."

She handed Mei-Lin a notebook ruled into two columns, headed Observed and Proposed. Mei-Lin wrote for twenty minutes. When she finished, the Observed column was longer, and she noticed that Ms. Achterberg seemed pleased by that.

Before they rehung the sampler, Ms. Achterberg removed the old typed label and set it carefully in a folder. She did not write a new one.$q$, 'draft')
