-- Passage rg12-story-details-inference-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('730fe2b5-2ffd-5f0f-bd80-34f061e7e099', 'rg12-story-details-inference-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Bell on Lake Tamsin$q$, $q$The fog came in faster than the forecast had promised. One minute Imani Brooks could see the far shore of Lake Tamsin, a dark line of pines; the next, she could barely make out the bow of her own boat, eight rowers ahead of her fading into gray.

As coxswain, Imani sat in the stern facing her crew, steering with two small cords and calling the stroke. She was the smallest person in the boat and the only one who could see where it was going, which, at the moment, was nowhere.

"Weigh enough," she called, and eight oars came off the water at once. The shell glided, slowed, and stopped.

Somewhere behind them, Coach Delgado's launch had been following with its motor humming. Now the humming coughed twice and went silent. They heard him pull the starter cord once, twice, a third time. Then they heard him sigh.

"Should we row back?" asked Theo, in the bow seat.

Imani considered it. The dock was perhaps half a mile north, but north was only a guess now. Somewhere along this shore were the rocks the crew called the Teeth, and last spring she had seen another boat's fin snapped clean off on them.

"We hold," she said. "Everybody quiet."

They sat in the silence. Water clicked against the hull. Then, faint and steady, came a sound she knew: the bell on the boathouse dock, which the groundskeeper rang every evening at six to call in the boats. It was not six yet.

Imani turned the rudder a few degrees toward the sound. "Bow pair only," she said. "Light pressure. Let's go find it."

They came in slowly, two oars at a time, the bell growing louder with every stroke. When the dock finally appeared, the groundskeeper was standing beside the bell with his phone in one hand. He lowered the rope when he saw them and did not say who had called him.$q$, 'draft')
