-- Passage rg12-story-details-inference-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('03ef8cd6-7294-5fa9-ad99-a5bca140ce10', 'rg12-story-details-inference-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-story-details-inference'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Stake 9$q$, $q$The stakes had been drilled into the glacier the previous September, twelve aluminum poles in a line running up the ice, each one marked in centimeters. Every summer someone from the field station walked the line and measured how much of each pole now stood above the surface. The more pole showing, the more ice had melted away around it.

This year that someone was Dr. Ngozi Eze and, for the first time, a student assistant: Anjali Mehta, seventeen, who had applied to the station's summer program mostly because it was the only one that accepted students who had not yet finished high school.

Stakes 1 through 8, lower on the ice, had shown between one and a half and two meters of melt. They reached Stake 9 a little after noon. It was not standing. It lay on its side on the ice, half buried in gritty snow, its base pointing downhill.

Anjali crouched beside it. "It melted out," she said. "The ice went down past where it was drilled, so it just fell over."

"Probably," Dr. Eze said. "What does that tell us?"

"That the melt here was more than the depth of the hole. So more than two meters." Anjali hesitated. "We could write two meters, since we know it was at least that."

Dr. Eze did not answer immediately. She photographed the stake from three angles, then opened the field book and turned it so Anjali could see. Under Stake 9 she wrote: Fallen. Melt exceeded 2.0 m. Exact value unknown.

"At least is not the same as equal to," she said, and handed Anjali the pencil to measure Stake 10.

Stakes 10 through 12, higher on the glacier, each showed less than one meter of melt. On the long walk back, Anjali was quiet. That night, in the bunkhouse, she opened the notebook she used for her own observations and wrote one line at the top of a clean page: What happened at 9? Then she sat looking at it for a long while and did not write anything underneath.$q$, 'draft')
