-- Passage rg12-text-structure-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('caa6c399-38fb-5090-a2af-db9254100c40', 'rg12-text-structure-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Why Your Receipt Fades$q$, $q$Anyone who has dug an old store receipt out of a coat pocket has seen it: the print has faded to a ghostly gray, or vanished entirely. The paper did not lose its ink, because it never had any. Understanding why receipts fade requires answering three questions, and the answers point to a small change in habit that could save many people trouble.

First, how does the print appear at all? Most receipts are printed on thermal paper, which is coated with a colorless dye mixed with a second chemical called a developer. The printer contains no ink. Instead, a row of tiny heating elements touches the paper, and wherever the coating is heated, the dye and developer react and turn dark.

Second, why does the print disappear? The reaction that darkens the coating can partly reverse. Heat, sunlight and contact with oils from skin or with certain plastics all speed that reversal. A receipt left on a car dashboard may fade in days, while one kept in a cool, dark drawer may last years.

Third, why does it matter? For most purchases it does not. But receipts also serve as proof of payment for warranties, returns and expense reports. A consumer survey by the Westbrook Household Finance Project found that nearly a third of respondents had at least once been unable to return an item or claim a repair because a receipt had become unreadable.

The fix is simple. For any receipt that might matter later, take a photograph of it or ask for an emailed copy on the day of purchase. A picture taken in ten seconds will outlast a paper slip that was never designed to survive.$q$, 'draft')
