-- Passage rg12-word-parts-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9a26d17e-2591-5be8-b3e2-5287ae4ca605', 'rg12-word-parts-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Salamander That Stays Young$q$, $q$Most amphibians lead two lives, one in water and one on land. A frog begins as a tadpole that breathes through gills, then undergoes a metamorphosis that reshapes nearly its entire body: legs sprout, the tail shrinks, and lungs take over from gills. The axolotl, a salamander native to a few lakes in central Mexico, is a striking exception. It usually skips this transformation altogether and spends its whole life in water, keeping the feathery gills and finned tail of a juvenile.

Biologists describe this condition as neoteny, the keeping of youthful features into adulthood. An adult axolotl can raise young of its own while still looking, in many ways, like an oversized larva. Under unusual laboratory conditions, an axolotl can be pushed into transforming into a land form, but in the wild this almost never happens.

The axolotl is even more famous for its ability to regenerate lost body parts. If it loses a limb, a mass of cells gathers at the wound. At first this cluster looks amorphous, with no recognizable structure, but over several weeks it organizes itself into bone, muscle, nerves, and skin. A complete, working limb eventually grows back, often without a visible scar.

Researchers hope that studying this process will help them understand why human tissue usually forms scars instead of rebuilding itself. The work matters for the axolotl, too. Its wild habitat has shrunk as the lakes have been drained and polluted, and conservationists now consider the species critically endangered. The animal that never grows up may depend on people who are willing to grow more careful.$q$, 'draft')
