  ('c94a744f-2392-53a5-9def-69f42fbb8d71', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-word-parts'), null, 'hard',
   $q$"Amphibian" comes from amphi ("both") and bio ("life"). Based on these word parts, why does the passage call the axolotl "a striking exception" among amphibians?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It lives both in water and on land, just as every frog does","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-contrast"},{"text":"It has two separate sets of gills that work at the same time","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"It lives only in water instead of leading both a water life and a land life","is_correct":true},{"text":"It can live twice as long as any other kind of salamander","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It lives only in water instead of leading both a water life and a land life$q$, $q$Amphibian means a creature with both lives, in water and on land, and the axolotl "spends its whole life in water," so it lacks the second life. Saying it lives both ways like a frog ignores the word "exception."$q$, 'draft', '9a26d17e-2591-5be8-b3e2-5287ae4ca605')
