-- Passage rg12-word-parts-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d0ad9405-d926-5787-b2f2-fa201e805e69', 'rg12-word-parts-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Upstream Leg$q$, $q$The head race on the Ashbourne River was famous for its tortuous course. Over three miles the river twisted through six sharp bends, and every coxswain knew that a poor line through the turns could cost a crew more time than any weakness in the rowers.

Adaeze Brennan had studied the course for weeks. As coxswain of her school's eight, she did not row; she steered, called the rhythm, and kept eight tired athletes believing in the plan. Before the race, she reminded them that a boat stays fast only when it keeps its equilibrium. "If one side pulls harder than the other, we wobble, and wobbling is slow," she said.

The crews launched at intervals of twenty seconds, each boat racing against the clock. Ahead of them, a rival crew drifted wide on the second bend, and Adaeze saw her chance. Rather than trailing behind, she chose to circumvent the slower boat by slipping past on the inside, where the current was weaker. It was a risky move, because the inside channel narrowed near a sandbar.

Halfway through, the rowers began to fade. Adaeze had saved one call for exactly this moment. She told them they were now equidistant from the start and the finish, and that everything they had trained for lived in the next mile and a half. The words seemed to revive the crew; the stroke rate climbed, and the boat surged forward.

They crossed the line exhausted. The results showed they had finished fourth, not first, but they had beaten their own best time by eleven seconds. Adaeze decided that was a victory worth recording in the team log.$q$, 'draft')
